hello mr brown thank you for joining us  on the programme this morning what are  you hoping for from this emergency  summit good morning  well we have a plan and we get the  vaccines out to those people who need  them so only two percent of africa  vaccinated only two percent of  low-income countries it's in our  interest in britain that they are  vaccinated because the disease will  spread in africa it will mutate and  there'll be new variants it will come  back to haunt even the fully vaccinated  in britain unless we take action so  we've been stopped by on vaccines we've  got a mountain of vaccines in america  and in europe  they're not going to be used even when  we do the boosters and the 12 to 15 year  olds there are still millions of  vaccines left over and we've got to get  them to the rest of the world otherwise  they're going to pass the use by date  and expire and be of no use to anybody  and all of us hate waste  so i don't understand why it's not as  easy as  opening up the belly of a british  airways jet putting them on board and  taking them down to joburg  well you've done charity work in africa  and you know that there's a desperate  need to get the vaccines into africa as  quickly as possible and that's what  should be being done and so in june they  had a plan that they would give does  share as they called it when they had  excess vaccines they'd pass them over  but it hasn't really happened enough and  yet there's all these vaccines lying in  warehouses or on contract for delivery  to britain america and europe and yet we  don't need them so we've got to get them  out as quickly as possible wednesday's  summit should agree a plan and it's in  everybody's interest our interest  to protect ourselves as well as to help  people in other countries do you have a  plan do you know how we should do it  yep my plan is that uh this month we  could transfer 300 million vaccines  that's from america and europe by next  month there could be 500 million and by  december a billion and so we need to get  all the resources at our disposal to  airlift them to get them out to the  countries that need them get the  vaccinations happening and provide some  support as you know okay from your  visits to africa  we need to give help to administer the  vaccines as well but that's something  we've also put in train and i've been  working with the world health  organization and other organizations to  make that happen as quickly as possible  do we have enough people because that  was going to be my next point you know  we're struggling as far as the nhs is  concerned here we haven't got any people  that we can send to put these jabs in  people's arms so what do we do we get  the vaccines there they're all there on  the runway then what do we do with them  well there's hundreds of thousands of  health workers in the low-income  countries and across africa they've not  even been vaccinated yet you know  to vaccinate all the health workers is a  huge task so that's going to happen  quickly because they're risking their  lives to save lives then we get to the  vulnerable elderly and then we get to  the rest of the population so that  africa and low-income countries can also  go back to work they're suffering  because  massive unemployment as well as massive  poverty as a result of this  i fear that unless we take this action  then africa could become the center for  covert and if in future you have new  variants and mutations coming out of  africa then it really will hurt the rest  of the world so it's in africa's  interests it's in our interest yes you  can devise a plan very quickly and  wednesday's summit they shouldn't go  around the table saying  congratulating themselves and what  they're doing they should go around the  table and say let's get a plan and let's  get all these surplus vaccines out there  and get them used because as i say what  i hate most of all about this is waste  because we've got genius of scientists  we've got the manufacturers in  delivering one and a half billion  vaccines a month and then they're lying  in warehouses  and not being used that's not good  enough and we in britain and people in  america and europe have got to get their  vaccines out to people who need them as  you say and and has often been quoted as  saying and nobody's safe unless  everybody's safe one wonders why all of  these vaccines are going to waste we  seem to have stalled here in the united  kingdom um in making sure that people  are double vaccinated i was looking at  the figures this morning 65 less than  two-thirds of the british public have  been fully vaccinated we used to lead  europe and now we're something like 7th  8th portugal spain lots of other  european countries ahead of us what's  happened  well in america and in europe as well as  in britain there is resistance among  some people to vaccinate and i want to  appeal to everybody who's not been  vaccinated  to get vaccinated and that helps them it  helps their families it helps protect  our communities so there is some  resistance and we've got to deal with  that but even if we set aside enough  vaccines for all who need them  and haven't asked for them yet and do  the boosters and do 3 million 12 to 15  year olds and get them vaccinated twice  which is 6 million vaccine vaccinations  we still have  millions of vaccines left over and by  christmas that will be 100 million if we  don't airlift them to africa and  elsewhere it could be 200 million  because of the delays in getting them to  people  and that is an excess above all the  other vaccinations that we still want to  see happen in the united kingdom  what do you think of these anti-vaxxers  with their bull horns who uh wander  around um shopping centers on a saturday  intimidating people and saying that it's  a government ploy to try and tag us  all well look i admire those people who  are not in politics and not really in  public life in the political sphere  who've gone out and said as individuals  like gareth southgate get vaccinated  and i think we need role models to  appeal to  young people and to the rest of the  community not to listen to these  anti-vexes because already we have  proved that a hundred thousand lives  have been saved in britain as a result  of vaccination  and if you think ahead to the next year  the estimate is that a million deaths  will happen across the world unless we  get vaccinations to people and that's a  huge figure of wasted lives as a result  of having wasted vaccines and so we  really have to act to persuade people to  get the vaccination but also to use the  vaccines we've got  sarah gilbert who was one of the  inventors of the astrozenica vaccine she  has been appealing to people to get the  vaccination and to get the vaccines out  to the rest of the world so people that  everybody can respect for their genius  and for their scientific achievement and  people in the world of sport and so on  are appealing to people to get  vaccinations i make that appeal today as  well  let me ask you um as a former prime  minister well i've got you've seen this  big argument that's developing between  us and the french at the moment because  um they had done a deal with the  australians to have um submarines  diesel-powered submarines and we've gone  behind their backs the french would say  and and done a deal uh with the  americans to to provide them with  nuclear submarines would you have done  it in that way um if you'd still been  prime minister  well i think we could have done it  better uh just as we could have done the  afghanistan exit a lot better and i  think there are lessons to learn and i  hope when boris johnson meets president  biden they'll discuss how the whole of  the west can work more closely together  but i think france also has an issue  here they want to if you like to have  strategic autonomy in europe and so they  will build this up as an argument  against working just with nato they want  a sort of french led european defense uh  capacity so i i think france has its own  motives for building this up but i think  we've got to learn to work better  together there is a standoff with china  at the moment if the west cannot unite  and say what it wants to see done then i  don't think we're going to be in a very  strong position to influence world  events in america and i think boris  johnson should say this to joe biden  tomorrow america used to act  unilaterally because it was a unipolar  age and they had complete hegemony now  we're in a multi-polar age and they've  got to work with other countries and  britain and america's partnership is  really important to that so i hope on  practical uh decisions that are made  tomorrow when boris johnson meets  president biden we can see the alliances  working far better than they did in  afghanistan  but how concerned are you that you know  our closest defense ally in the form of  the french i've withdrawn their  ambassadors from australia and also from  the us and have pulled out of defense  talks with the united kingdom later on  in the week now you know they're french  they might be a little bit sulky about  it but they are our closest defense  allies we do need to work with them  while uh organizations like the  americans are keeping an eye on what's  happening in the far east  yeah well as i've said i think this is  more to do with french domestic politics  i think president macron is asserting  the importance that he attaches to  french  involvement in the defense of the west  and the defense of the world and i think  this will blow over pretty pretty  quickly in my view but what we've got to  watch is that there's a split over time  between the french wanting to take  europe one way  and the americans britain and nato  wanting to go another way i think that's  something we must leave it there mr  brown i'm so sorry we're out of time  thank you for joining us on the  programme this morning

